WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.033
- This is Sound Field.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.070
- We all scream, maybe
from happiness, anger,

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.977
fear, shock, pain, but why
so much screaming in metal?

00:00:12.977 --> 00:00:15.680
And how do we do it without
destroying our voice?

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.401
Nahre and I are gonna find out.

00:00:17.401 --> 00:00:20.570
(dramatic music)

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.540
- We'll likely never know

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.010
when screaming and
music first intersected,

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.690
but the best guess is that it occurred

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.980
in tribal music from around the world.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.290
After hearing a common
Viking vocalization,

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.510
a 10th century Arab traveler
in Denmark said this,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.720
never before have I heard uglier songs

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.220
than those of the Vikings.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.430
The growling sound
coming from their throats

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.343
reminds me of dogs
howling, only more untamed.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.950
- Sounds pretty metal, right?

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.410
Screaming has appeared in operas

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.486
and avant garde compositions
going back to the 1800s.

00:00:49.486 --> 00:00:50.650
♪ Ah ♪

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.820
- But for its first
appearance in pop music,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.550
many people point to blues
musicians who often had to yell

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.640
over the crowds in the clubs they played.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.980
One example is a song
deemed too scary for radio,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.972
Screamin' Jay Hawkins' 1956
classic, I Put a Spell on You.

00:01:04.972 --> 00:01:09.972
♪ Because you're mine ♪

00:01:10.294 --> 00:01:13.010
♪ Oh yeah ♪

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.300
- But the real groundwork for metal

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.640
came with Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.250
Both bands took the blues
and made it heavier,

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.170
adding theatrics, and of course,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.276
the howling of Ozzy
Osbourne and Robert Plant.

00:01:24.276 --> 00:01:26.776
♪ Ah ah ah ah ♪

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.890
- That's when metal
screaming really took off,

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.810
whether it was the barroom mania of AC/DC,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.680
the leather-clad
intimidation of Judas Priest,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.120
or the black metal bands of Europe.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.210
I don't have that much
knowledge about metal, hardcore,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.982
what is it, metalcore?

00:01:43.982 --> 00:01:45.042
- Metal
- Hardcore?

00:01:45.042 --> 00:01:45.930
Metal.
- or hardcore music.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.965
Metal, yeah, I don't know
that much about metal.

00:01:48.965 --> 00:01:51.659
("Deceiver" by Judas Priest)

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:53.270
♪ Oh, let me tell you ♪

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.093
- This is, I like this head voice.

00:01:56.028 --> 00:01:56.861
♪ Eh eh eh ♪

00:01:56.861 --> 00:02:00.160
- That first bit was
quite, maybe not beautiful,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.950
but it was quite refined.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.089
- He had vibrato going real high.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.477
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- I thought that was tight.

00:02:04.477 --> 00:02:06.170
♪ I was shaking at the knees ♪

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.317
- It's so recognizable too, that--

00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:09.150
- Yeah.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.640
- It's specific tone of screaming.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.473
- [LA] Yep.

00:02:12.473 --> 00:02:14.900
- I don't think I've ever
screamed like that ever, even.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.657
Just in life.

00:02:17.657 --> 00:02:18.740
- I can see how.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.535
(Corey screaming)

00:02:20.535 --> 00:02:21.368
- Whoa.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:25.280
The areas that I did notice in metal

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.110
were the virtuosity of the players,

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.050
the guitarists, the drummers.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.064
- Metal drumming, double
bass pedal, and like,

00:02:34.064 --> 00:02:35.312
how fast you have to be.
- Super intense.

00:02:35.312 --> 00:02:36.420
- Yeah, how fast you have to be.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.610
- But I never really
thought about the screaming.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.790
And I just thought, okay
that's just part of the genre,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.240
but now that I think about it,

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.810
I'm sure it takes a lot of
energy, a lot of technique.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.370
There seems to be more to it

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.800
than I previously thought maybe.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.180
I think it'd be hard to sing
and scream and growl that way

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.750
while, you know, banging your head though.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:00.823
Kind of up and down?

00:03:02.065 --> 00:03:06.391
- They do it though, they do it, like.

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:08.667
(Natalie screaming)

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:09.980
- So, that's kind of how you do it.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.030
- Keep it.
(Natalie chuckles)

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.090
That was cold.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.244
- That was cool.
- Cool, I'm glad you all,

00:03:15.244 --> 00:03:16.200
glad you all liked that.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.630
When you're playing aggressive music,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.590
you have to give an
aggressive performance.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.580
For me, it's like this is
a very emotional experience

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:23.950
playing this sort of music.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:26.110
- So, how do these metal vocalists scream?

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.535
Well, they basically
do it just like babies.

00:03:28.535 --> 00:03:30.350
(baby crying)

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.810
Dr. Krzysztof Izdebski

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.130
of the Pacific Voice and Speech Foundation

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:38.020
told Inside Science, a little
baby has all the sounds,

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.630
it has the sounds of scream and growl,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.900
and inhalation and high pitch,
and whistle and low pitch.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.676
- I'm not really a metal kid,
I'm just kind of a den mother.

00:03:47.676 --> 00:03:50.509
(Melissa screams)

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:55.740
What I just did is I made
my true vocal folds flutter.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.230
When you speak, your vocal folds,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.600
which are at the top of your windpipe,

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.420
they come together like this, right?

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.050
They're actually horizontal in your body,

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.580
but I'm gonna do it like
this so you can see better.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.120
When we talk, they do this.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.520
When we sing, they do waves,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.600
cycles per second, they're pitches, right?

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.730
In other words, this is like a 440, right?

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.730
If you're singing A, that is
440 sound waves per second.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.540
When I sing that note,
my vocal folds vibrate

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.700
440 times per second, right?

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.700
But in screaming, it's not
a repeating periodic wave,

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.330
it's chaos, it's a
flutter, it's like a fart,

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.467
it's like (blows raspberry) like that.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:41.720
- What's the maintenance, or
like, what are the exercises

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.270
you do so you don't--
- Oh, I just do normal vocal

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.220
warm-ups, like, entirely just your basic

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.926
run of the mill vocal warm-ups.

00:04:47.926 --> 00:04:48.759
- Okay.

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:51.350
- First two shows that I ever
did doing vocals like this,

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.430
I like, destroyed my voice.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:53.880
- Word.
- And you know,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.560
I haven't blown out my voice since then

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:56.740
'cause I figured out, like okay,

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.600
well this is just how you do it, and--

00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.433
- You take care of your voice.
- Yeah.

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:01.700
Just like, not like you're
just gonna go for a run

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.130
and like, be like okay,
I can run a marathon now,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.750
and I'm just gonna do
this and I'm gonna be fine

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:06.630
the next time, no you have to like,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.570
warm up, you have to stretch,
you have to practice.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.450
For instance, opera singers also like,

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:12.710
really push their voice.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.260
I could guarantee you that we
sort of all use our diaphragms

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:16.620
in very similar ways.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.700
I just learned how to use
it a little bit differently.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:21.533
- Add your own texture.

00:05:21.533 --> 00:05:22.366
- Exactly.

00:05:22.366 --> 00:05:23.860
- Okay.
- You can't imitate this.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.650
When you imitate, you're
in your left brain, right?

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.140
That's the brain that does math problems.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.830
But like, when you were
a kid on the playground

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.550
and you were going (mimics
siren wailing) I'm a fireman,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.820
(mimics siren wailing)

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.920
you're just like, straight up right brain.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.690
So, I have to take all
the technical information

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.210
and put it in the right brain,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.130
and that's how it gets super wacky

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.700
because I say okay, pretend you're a cat.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:45.870
(mimics cat meowing)

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.184
Pretend you're a cat that has laryngitis.

00:05:48.184 --> 00:05:49.017
(mimics raspy cat meowing)

00:05:49.017 --> 00:05:49.850
Right?

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:51.720
You know, but you cannot
think about your diaphragm,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.620
you can't think of air in your windpipe

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.990
because there's no neural steering wheels

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.760
to close your vocal folds
in the front or the back.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.750
I just pretend there's
particles coming out of my eyes

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:04.867
and then I go (screams).

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:07.840
- The larger the larynx and lungs,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.350
the greater your potential
is for a louder scream.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.170
Some people are just built better for it,

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:15.459
like Jill Drake, a classroom
assistant from the UK.

00:06:15.459 --> 00:06:18.720
(Jill screams)

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.670
That's 129 decibels, almost
as loud as a gunshot,

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.670
and definitely powerful
enough to damage your hearing.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.210
Okay, so screaming is a
skill, but how do they compare

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.970
to the great singers like
Whitney Houston or Mariah Carey?

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.668
Take Faith No More's
Mike Patton for example.

00:06:34.668 --> 00:06:36.830
(Mike screams)

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.920
His vocal range is
reportedly over six octaves,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.513
going from E flat one to to E seven.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.660
Compare that to Mariah Carey's range

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.903
of G sharp two to G seven.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:55.050
- So, you don't scream
because you can't sing,

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.230
screaming is just an expression of rage.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.540
And it's wonderful.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.620
See, it's not a skill that you develop

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.130
because you can't do anything else.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.840
Who cares if it's not music?

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.320
Okay, it's theater, okay,
whatever it is, it's cool.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.610
Most importantly, it's a connection.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:17.980
It is an opportunity to connect,

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.980
and that's what music really is,

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.440
no matter what the genre is, right?

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.430
- The early 2000s saw the
rise of a number of groups

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.390
that blended thrash and hardcore metal

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.530
with punk and alternative rock

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:34.230
for mixtures of harshness
and complex melodies.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:38.770
The genres were known as
metalcore, screamo, post-hardcore,

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.980
and whatever else bands and
their fans decided to call them.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.497
Bands like Underoath.

00:07:43.497 --> 00:07:45.023
♪ I swear we need to find some ♪

00:07:45.023 --> 00:07:46.928
- [Nahre] Bullet for My Valentine.

00:07:46.928 --> 00:07:48.900
♪ There's always something
different going on ♪

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.146
- [Nahre] Bring Me the Horizon.

00:07:50.146 --> 00:07:52.580
♪ Can you feel my heart ♪

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.140
- [Nahre] And dozens more cut their teeth

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.150
on the Warped Tour.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:57.320
- All right, you wanna call it this time?

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.270
Rock Paper Scissors, shoot?

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.960
- Okay, one, two, three.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:02.964
Go.
- Boom.

00:08:02.964 --> 00:08:05.780
♪ Oh my God ♪

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:06.800
- [LA] Nahre and I are gonna listen

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.040
to the screaming vocal track

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.430
of a mystery song chosen by our producers,

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:11.673
and we're gonna try to
create our own music

00:08:11.673 --> 00:08:13.836
that we think would fit under it.

00:08:13.836 --> 00:08:16.249
(singer screaming)

00:08:16.249 --> 00:08:18.760
♪ I wear them proudly ♪

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:19.910
- What are they saying?

00:08:21.002 --> 00:08:22.430
♪ Whoa ♪

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.130
- Okay.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:25.312
We got a key.

00:08:25.312 --> 00:08:28.350
♪ Clock counting down ♪

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.604
- Okay, so it goes like.

00:08:29.604 --> 00:08:32.330
(light piano music)

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:35.280
I'm so clueless, I don't
even know where to start.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.210
- If we came from like,
an avant garde angle,

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.420
we don't know what the original,

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:40.650
I'm sure the original is just like.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:41.943
♪ Da da da da da da da da da ♪

00:08:41.943 --> 00:08:43.400
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.880
Like, if we came left field with it.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:50.670
- Mm-hmm, let's try to just
find ways to use our resources.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:51.503
- [LA] Yeah.

00:08:51.503 --> 00:08:55.700
- And see if we can tap into
that kind of primal energy.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.410
A lot of motives that I hear in metal

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.520
also are in Baroque music.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:02.411
- Really?

00:09:02.411 --> 00:09:03.861
- [Nahre] Like all the.

00:09:03.861 --> 00:09:05.441
(frantic piano music)

00:09:05.441 --> 00:09:06.274
- [LA] That!

00:09:06.274 --> 00:09:07.547
That!
- That's Vivaldi!

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:08.380
- All of that!

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.130
That's what I'm talking
about, all of that.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.360
- You know that's Baroque,
that's Baroque music.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.310
- We gotta get, see, yeah,

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.505
we gotta get all that into the draft.

00:09:15.505 --> 00:09:17.519
♪ Stand up and scream ♪

00:09:17.519 --> 00:09:19.684
(Nahre laughs)

00:09:19.684 --> 00:09:20.641
- Yeah, that's right.

00:09:20.641 --> 00:09:21.875
(LA laughs)

00:09:21.875 --> 00:09:22.858
(thunder crashes)

00:09:22.858 --> 00:09:25.191
♪ Oh my God ♪

00:09:26.328 --> 00:09:29.245
(singer screaming)

00:09:39.622 --> 00:09:40.930
- [Nahre] And now that
you've heard our version,

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.565
let's listen to the original.

00:09:42.565 --> 00:09:44.065
- Three, two, one.

00:09:48.731 --> 00:09:49.564
- Oh.

00:09:49.564 --> 00:09:51.889
- I knew it was gonna be a six, I knew it.

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:54.991
♪ Oh my God ♪

00:09:54.991 --> 00:09:56.491
- Oh, interesting.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:59.723
I totally hear it now.

00:09:59.723 --> 00:10:03.801
♪ If only he knew ♪

00:10:03.801 --> 00:10:05.336
♪ If only he knew ♪

00:10:05.336 --> 00:10:07.501
♪ Just stand up and scream ♪

00:10:07.501 --> 00:10:10.330
♪ The tainted clock is counting down ♪

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.433
♪ Da da da dum da da da dum ♪

00:10:12.433 --> 00:10:16.207
- I mean, you have to be
very on top of your playing.

00:10:16.207 --> 00:10:19.227
